THOUGHT: Fields `a` and `c` are non-final and only set in `doSort`, but NullAway likely treats them as @NonNull and requires initialization in the constructor. A safe fix is to initialize them to benign non-null defaults in the no-arg constructor (e.g., empty array and a comparator that throws if used before proper setup). I’ll edit TimSort.java to assign `a` and `c` in the constructor, then run the NullAway build to confirm the error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/libgdx && sed -i '115,119c\  TimSort() {
    tmp = (T[]) new Object[INITIAL_TMP_STORAGE_LENGTH];
    // Initialize to safe non-null defaults; real values are set in doSort
    a = (T[]) new Object[0];
    c = new Comparator<T>() {
      @Override
      public int compare(T o1, T o2) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("Comparator not initialized");
      }
    };
    runBase = new int[40];
    runLen = new int[40];
  }' gdx/src/com/badlogic/gdx/utils/TimSort.java && ./gradlew gdx:compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E 'NullAway' new_build_output.log | tail -n 5 || tail -n 40 new_build_output.log
```

